# AL MCP Server — Tool Catalog and Usage Rules

The plugin wires `altool launchmcpserver --transport stdio` as the `al` MCP server (see `mcp.json`). The server requires `altool` on PATH, which ships with either the VS Code AL Language extension or the `Microsoft.Dynamics.BusinessCentral.Development.Tools` NuGet package (provides the `al` alias for headless agents).

## Server identifier (READ FIRST — common first-call mistake)

When the agent has access to a meta tool that calls MCP servers by name (e.g. `CallMcpTool(server=..., toolName=..., arguments=...)`), the `server` argument must be the **identifier**, not the friendly name:

| Surface                    | Value to use                                       |
| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Friendly name (docs, UI)   | `al`                                               |
| Cursor server identifier   | `plugin-business-central-al-al`                    |

The identifier prefix is `plugin-<plugin-name>-`, which Cursor adds automatically when a plugin defines an MCP server. Source of truth: `~/.cursor/projects/<workspace-id>/mcps/plugin-business-central-al-al/SERVER_METADATA.json` contains `{"serverIdentifier": "plugin-business-central-al-al", "serverName": "al"}`.

**Never pass `server="al"` to a meta MCP tool.** That will fail with `MCP server does not exist: al`. Use `server="plugin-business-central-al-al"`.

If the agent's MCP tools are exposed as already-prefixed functions (e.g. `mcp_plugin-business-central-al-al_al_build(...)`), call them directly — no server argument needed.

## Tool catalog

| Tool                        | What it does                                                                       |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `al_build`                  | Compile the AL project and produce a `.app`.                                       |
| `al_compile`                | Compile WITHOUT producing `.app`. Faster validation. MCP-only.                     |
| `al_publish`                | Publish the extension to a cloud or on-prem BC environment.                        |
| `al_downloadsymbols`        | Download dependent symbol packages.                                                |
| `al_symbolsearch`           | Search AL symbols across the project + dependencies. **See critical quirk below.** |
| `al_getdiagnostics`         | Retrieve filtered compilation diagnostics.                                         |
| `al_getpackagedependencies` | List `app.json` dependencies. MCP-only.                                            |
| `al_auth_login`             | Interactive MSAL Entra ID sign-in. MCP-only.                                       |
| `al_auth_logout`            | Clear cached Entra ID tokens. MCP-only.                                            |

## Critical quirk — `al_symbolsearch` parameter wrapping

`al_symbolsearch` is the **only** tool that requires its arguments to be wrapped inside a `parameters` key. Every other tool accepts arguments at the top level. Forgetting this is the single most common mistake when calling the AL MCP.

Correct shape for `al_symbolsearch`:

```json
{
  "parameters": {
    "query": "Post",
    "filters": {
      "kinds": ["Codeunit"],
      "scope": "project"
    }
  }
}
```

Correct shape for every other tool (example `al_build`):

```json
{
  "scope": "current",
  "onlyErrors": true,
  "enableCodeAnalysis": true,
  "codeAnalyzers": ["${CodeCop}", "${AppSourceCop}"]
}
```

## Code analyzer placeholders

Pass these literal placeholder strings (with the dollar-brace) when enabling analyzers via `al_build.codeAnalyzers` or `al_compile.codeAnalyzers`:

- `${CodeCop}`
- `${AppSourceCop}`
- `${PerTenantExtensionCop}`
- `${UICop}`

## Diagnostic codes and area filters

`al_getdiagnostics` returns diagnostics with stable `code` strings (e.g. `AL0118`, `AS0023`, `AA0074`). The `areas` filter accepts `"AL"`, `"AppSourceCop"`, `"CodeCop"`, `"PerTenantExtensionCop"`, `"UICop"`. The `severities` filter accepts `"error"`, `"warning"`, `"info"`, `"hint"`. Limit defaults to 200, max 500.

## Authentication

`al_auth_login` is MCP-only and uses browser-based MSAL for Entra ID. Tokens are cached. Required before `al_publish` or `al_downloadsymbols` against a cloud environment. NOT required when:

- `al_downloadsymbols` is called with `globalSourcesOnly: true` (pulls from MS NuGet feeds and AppSource — ideal for CI and offline).
- `al_publish` targets an on-prem server with Windows authentication.

Pass `noCache: true` on any tool to force a fresh sign-in. Call `al_auth_logout` to clear cached tokens.

## Canonical workflows (use these compositions)

### Validate-only (PR gate, fast)

```text
al_downloadsymbols  (globalSourcesOnly: true)
al_compile          (onlyErrors: true)
al_getdiagnostics   (severities: ["error"])
```

### Full build and deploy to sandbox

```text
al_auth_login       (tenant, environmentType: "Sandbox", environmentName)
al_downloadsymbols
al_build            (scope: "current")
al_publish          (environmentName, environmentType, tenant)
```

### AppSourceCop quality gate

```text
al_compile          (enableCodeAnalysis: true, codeAnalyzers: ["${AppSourceCop}"])
al_getdiagnostics   (areas: ["AppSourceCop"])
```

## `al_publish` schema-update modes

`schemaUpdateMode` accepts `"Synchronize"` (default), `"ForceSync"`, or `"Recreate"`. **Never default to `"Recreate"`** in compound commands — it drops data. Reserve it for explicit user-initiated resets.

## Project-path arguments

When the workspace contains multiple AL projects, pass `projectPath` (absolute path to the `app.json` folder) on `al_build`, `al_compile`, `al_publish`, `al_downloadsymbols`, `al_getdiagnostics`, and `al_getpackagedependencies`. Without it, the tools target "the first project in the workspace" which can be wrong.

## Initial project load — call `al_addproject` if no projects are loaded

The plugin's default `mcp.json` launches `altool launchmcpserver` with **no positional `<projects>` argument**, so a fresh session starts with **zero projects loaded**. Any tool that operates on AL code (`al_build`, `al_compile`, `al_symbolsearch`, `al_getdiagnostics`, `al_getpackagedependencies`) will return an empty/no-project result until at least one project is registered.

Before issuing the first project-scoped tool call in a session, the agent must either:

1. Verify a workspace-level `.cursor/mcp.json` exists that already injects the project path (e.g. `"args": [..., "${workspaceFolder}"]`) — in that case, projects are pre-loaded and no `al_addproject` is needed.
2. Call `al_addproject` with the absolute path to the workspace's `app.json` folder once, before any other tool call.

If `al_addproject` is needed every session, suggest the user run `/al-setup` — Step 4 of that skill offers to write a workspace-level `.cursor/mcp.json` that bakes in `${workspaceFolder}` so this becomes a one-time setup per project.

